aom 8-bit byte-identity across the 10/12-bit wiring
===================================================

Question: did wiring 10- and 12-bit into the zenav1-aom encode seam move any
8-bit output byte?

Method: a source snapshot of the PRE-change tree (`git archive ec6728b`) and
the post-change tree each drive the SAME emitter
(`dev/downstream-probe/src/bin/bd8_anchor.rs`), which encodes 60 cells and
prints length + fnv1a-64 of the whole AVIF file per cell. The two TSVs are
diffed.

Grid (60 cells): 6 geometries {64x64, 33x47, 128x96, 192x64, 65x33, 256x256}
x 5 (quality, speed) pairs {(90,6), (80,1), (80,10), (20,6), (99,4)}
x 2 content classes {gradient, flat 235}.
Quality 100 is excluded on purpose: `--cq-level 0` PANICS upstream on flat
content (zenavif#45).

Box: Apple M4 Pro, macOS 25.5.0. Base rev ec6728b (0.1.8); head = the 0.2.0
high-bit-depth commits.

RESULT
------
60/60 cells byte-identical (length AND hash) between pre- and post-change.

ANTI-VACUITY, measured the same way
-----------------------------------
Mutating the post-change tree's 8-bit conversion from YuvRange::Limited to
YuvRange::Full changes 60/60 cells. So the harness detects a change, and the
identity above is a result rather than a broken comparison.

SECOND RESULT, unexpected and worth keeping
-------------------------------------------
Disabling the u8-kernel special case in `color_planes_420` — routing 8-bit
input at depth 8 through the depth-generic `rgbx_to_yuv420_u16` recipe like
every other cell — changes 0/60 cells. The two kernels agree byte-for-byte at
output depth 8 on this corpus.

That means the special case is NOT what keeps 8-bit output stable, contrary to
what the first draft of its comment claimed. It is kept as a conservatism and
performance choice (the u8 kernel packs twice the lanes per vector), not as a
correctness guard. Nobody has measured the speed difference, so that half is
stated as a reason to keep, not as a claim about magnitude.
